    \u0438\u0441\u0447\u0438\u0441\u043b\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044e. \u0414\u0436\u043e\u043d \u0423\u043e\u043b\u043b\u0438\u0441
    \u0441\u043a\u043e\u043d\u0447\u0430\u043b\u0441\u044f \u0432 \u041e\u043a\u0441\u0444\u043e\u0440\u0434\u0435
    28 \u043e\u043a\u0442\u044f\u0431\u0440\u044f 1703 \u0433\u043e\u0434\u0430 \u0438 \u0431\u044b\u043b
    \u043f\u043e\u0445\u043e\u0440\u043e\u043d\u0435\u043d \u0432 \u0446\u0435\u0440\u043a\u0432\u0438
    \u0421\u0432\u044f\u0442\u043e\u0439 \u041c\u0430\u0440\u0438\u0438."
  shortBioEn: "John Wallis is one of the key figures in the history of mathematics, which was at the source of mathematical analysis and paved the way for Isaac Newton, and his life was closely intertwined with the dramatic events of the 17th century English history, and his diverse talents extended far beyond pure science.\n\n==Early years and education==John Wallace was born on November 23, 1616 in Ashford, Kent County, in a family of priests, and his father died when John was only six years old, but his family was able to give him a good primary education. Wallis was early in his math skills, with phenomenal memory and numeracy skills, and he was legendaryly able to extract square roots from 53 digits in his mind.However, he did not receive a systematic math education, mostly on his own, and his interest in mathematics woke up at the age of 15, when he accidentally saw his brother's book on arithmetic and learned it in two weeks. In 1632, Wallace went to Emmanuel College at Cambridge University, where he received his bachelor's degree in 1637 and master's degree in 1640.In Cambridge, he also studied medicine and, with courage, publicly defended William Garway's new theory of blood circulation, and after graduating from the university, he took a spiritual sanctuary and became a priest of the Church of England.\n\nIn 1645, Wallace married Susanne Glade, forcing him to leave Cambridge, as professors and researchers at the time had to observe the vow of celibacy, and during that period his career took a sudden turn, and during the English Civil War Wallis, who supported parliamentarians, became famous as an expert cryptographer, deciphering the oversold letters of the King's supporters.This talent brought him political protection, and his keen interest in mathematics started in London in 1647, after reading William Otred's work \"Clavis Mathematics\" (Clavis Mathematicae), and he quickly mastered modern mathematics and began his own research. Despite his collaboration with parliamentarians, Wallis opposed the execution of King Charles I.However, his reputation as a scientist was so high that in 1649 he was appointed as the Sabelian Professor of Geometry at the University of Oxford, replacing the expelled Royalist, who held the Chair until his death in 1703.\n\nThe years of Oxford have been the most fruitful in Wallace's life. In 1655, he published his main work, \"Aritmetica Infinitorum\", which was the most important step in the history of the integrated calculation, and Wallis: introduced into mathematics the symbol of infinity, which is still used.The systematization and development of the \"undissolved\" method of Cavalry, giving it an algebraic shape, found the squares under the curves, essentially calculating the integrals for the higher function \\(y = x\\m\\), including for fractional and negative indicators, and produced a famous infinite product for the number \\(pi\\) known as Wallis' formula. In 1657, his \"Mathesis Universalis\" was published in algebra and geometry.In 1659, he published \"Tactat on conical sections\", where he first defined these curves not through geometric constructions in cone, but through algebraic equations in the second order. In 1685, Wallis published an extensive \"Tactate on algebra.\" This work was a true encyclopedia of his time, in which the logarithm theory was presented, where logarithm was first defined as an operation, a reverse climbing to the degree.Negative and fractional degrees were used and developed, and terms such as \"mantiss\" and \"continuous fraction\" were introduced.\n\n==History==The social recognition and multifaceted talent of Wallace was one of the founders and first members of the Royal Society of London (1662). After the restoration of the monarchy, he was able to gain the trust of King Charles II, who even appointed him as his court priest. Interestingly, Wallace's scientific interests were not limited to mathematics.He was a linguist, writing a tract on the grammar of English, and developed one of the first systems for teaching deaf speech, writing logic and theology, and Wallace was famous for his complex character and many scientific disputes, including with Thomas Gobbs and Christian Guygens, and his work had a tremendous impact on young Isaac Newton, who, by his own admission, studied Wallis's work in Cambridge.It was in letters to Wallace Newton that he first presented his ideas on differential calculation, and John Wallace died in Oxford on October 28, 1703 and was buried in the church of St. Mary."
  interestingFactsRu: "\u041b\u0438\u0447\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0442\u044c \u0414\u0436\u043e\u043d\u0430 \u0423\u043e\u043b\u043b\u0438\u0441\u0430
    \u0431\u044b\u043b\u0430 \u043f\u043e\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u043a\u043e\u043d\u0442\u0440\u0430\u0441\u0442\u043e\u0432
    \u0438 \u043d\u0435\u043e\u0436\u0438\u0434\u0430\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0435\u0439.
